<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jack London</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a>–<a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as John Griffith London</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> was an American novelist, journalist and activist, one of the first American authors to become an international celebrity and earn a large fortune from writing. After leaving the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, Berkeley, in 1897 he joined the Klondike Gold Rush, where he developed scurvy; the north became the setting of The Call of the Wild, White Fang and the story To Build a Fire. A member of the radical literary group The Crowd in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, he advocated socialism and workers' rights in works such as The Iron Heel and The People of the Abyss. In 1905 he bought a ranch at Glen Ellen in Sonoma County, where he died in 1916, suffering from uremia and late-stage alcoholism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jack-london.jpg" alt="Jack London" width="330" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: published by L C Page and Company Boston 1903 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jack_London_young.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
